a compound assignment to a bitfield is a read-modify-write.

IT LOADED THE WHOLE UNIT, APPLIED THE OPERATOR, AND STORED THE WHOLE UNIT:

    str_x1,[x18,-8]!    # compound operator
    ldr_x1,[x1]         # the whole unit, not the field
    orr_x0,x1,x0
    ldr_x1,[x18],8      # compound operator
    strh_w0,[x1]        # no mask, no shift

so `field |= x` landed on BIT ZERO of the unit instead of on the field. The
plain `=` path has masked and shifted for a long time; only `|=`, `&=`, `+=`
and the rest were missing it -- which is why the emitted code AROUND them
looked correct and this took several passes to see.

WHAT IT COST, AND IT IS THREE SYMBOLS IN ONE LINK. tcc merges symbol
attributes with

    sa->weak |= sa1->weak;                      tccgen.c, merge_symattr

and `weak` is bit 6 of a sixteen-bit unit, so the bit never arrived and
put_extern_sym2 chose STB_GLOBAL over STB_WEAK. musl declares three symbols
hidden in a header and weak-aliases each in one translation unit while another
defines it for real:

    __acquire_ptc     pthread_impl.h + pthread_create.c + lock_ptc.c
    __release_ptc     pthread_impl.h + pthread_create.c + lock_ptc.c
    __malloc_atfork   fork.c + mallocng/glue.h

With the weak attribute lost, all three were emitted GLOBAL and collided:

    libc.a: error: '__acquire_ptc' defined twice
                   '__release_ptc' defined twice
                   '__malloc_atfork' defined twice

That is where GNU make stopped at rung 3.5 of the stage-4 ladder, with all 27
of its objects compiled and only the link left.

MEASURED, on the four-line reduction, either side:

    __attribute__((__visibility__("hidden"))) void __acquire_ptc(void);
    static void dummy_0(void) { }
    extern __typeof(dummy_0) __acquire_ptc __attribute__((__weak__, __alias__("dummy_0")));

    gcc-built tcc, same pin and patches   WEAK
    mc-tcc before                         GLOBAL
    mc-tcc after                          WEAK

AND IT IS NOT ONLY tcc's ATTRIBUTES. Every `|=`, `&=`, `^=`, `+=`, `-=` on a
bitfield anywhere was writing to the wrong bits, silently. tcc keeps its
function attributes in the same shape, and so does mallocng's struct meta.

AND EVERY BITFIELD EMIT NOW GOES THROUGH THE ARCHITECTURE HELPERS. Both the
plain and the compound store emitted `and_x0,x1,x0`, `orr_x0,x1,x0`,
`lsl_x0,x1,x0` and `lsr_x0,x1,x0` LITERALLY -- fourteen sites -- so a bitfield
write emitted aarch64 mnemonics on every architecture. Nothing in the corpus
wrote a bitfield, so amd64 never noticed: the case added with this patch is the
first, and it failed there until the strings were routed through
bitwise_and_instruction, bitwise_or_instruction and two new shift helpers whose
per-architecture strings are the ones compound_operation already selects for
`<<` and unsigned `>>`.

WHY THE FIX IS SHAPED LIKE THIS. The field description is captured BEFORE the
right-hand side is evaluated, because evaluating it may touch another bitfield
and overwrite the globals -- the same reason the plain assignment captures
them first. The old field is then extracted into REGISTER_ONE without
disturbing REGISTER_ZERO, so the operator sees exactly the two operands it
would for an ordinary member, and the store is the same read-modify-write the
plain path emits.

